From fe09b89b60f711870e4659c0c3e095ac6736febf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.local>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 19:50:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] A rail that halts all trading must not be able to do so
 quietly

Re-lands the seal-classification fix the 17:12Z adoption reverted (origin was 506 commits
behind the box, so Adopt-Release wrote origin's older blobs over it), and adds the fence that
would have made the original outage audible on the day it started.

WHAT WAS SILENT. `release_identity` refused every gold placement for seventeen days. The desk's
only record was 1,200 rows in `decision_ledger.jsonl` -- 583 in one day, one a minute, each
reading `release_identity_refused`. No alert, no dashboard, no human; the principal found it by
eye. Every other signal was green: the gateway alive and reconciling each minute, a current
`last_reconcile`, the task table Ready. An absence of orders looks exactly like a quiet market.
Cost: 19 gold sleeve-days lost against 22 placed since 2026-09-07T07:35:15Z.

`check_placement_interlock.py` reads the HALT, not its cause: a run of one reason repeating for
one sleeve with no placement in between. Deliberately not keyed to `release_identity_refused` --
the next halt will carry a different string and a fence built around today's would be just as
silent for it. Thirty rows or one hour. The VENUE's own answers (broker_rejected,
entry_inside_freeze_band, margin_guard) are reported, never breached on: a rail doing its job is
not a bug. It caps nothing and gates no capital. Run against the live ledger it fails at once and
names all eight halted sleeves, gold_london_am at 416 consecutive refusals over 7.3h.

A FAILED DELIVERY IS NOT A REASON TO STOP THE BOOK. `Adopt-And-Seal.ps1` exited the moment
`Adopt-Release` returned non-zero, so any delivery failure -- at 16:45Z it was `fatal: cannot
lock ref 'HEAD'`, a lock race, with nothing wrong with the tree -- left the release unsealed, and
an unsealed release makes the gateway refuse new risk every pass for the rest of the day. It now
seals anyway and carries the delivery failure out in its exit code, so the task history and
`check_adoption_freshness` still redden. This is step 5's own argument applied one stage earlier:
an unsealed tree is "a halt bought from a slow or unrelated-red gate. That reduces the book by
fiat, which the principal's standing order (2026-09-08) forbids." It is not a weakening -- a
half-written tree leaves modified CODE paths uncommitted and `seal_blocking_paths()` refuses on
exactly those, so the dangerous case is measured directly rather than inferred from an exit code.

